    <section class="py-16 border-t border-gray-100">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4">
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mb-8">Frequently asked questions on PFAS analysis</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group" open>
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">Which PFAS limits apply to drinking water in Germany?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Since 12 January 2026, the sum of 20 PFAS (Sum of PFAS-20) is limited to 0.0001 mg/l, i.e. 0.1 µg/l or 100 ng/l. From 12 January 2028, an additional limit of 0.00002 mg/l, i.e. 20 ng/l, applies to the sum of PFOA, PFNA, PFHxS and PFOS (Sum of PFAS-4). Both values are set out in Annex 2 of the German Drinking Water Ordinance (TrinkwV).</div>
            </details>
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group">
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">What limit of quantification must a laboratory achieve for PFAS in drinking water?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Under Annex 7 of the TrinkwV, the limit of quantification must not exceed 30 per cent of the parametric value. For the sum parameters this is applied to the individual substances: each one must be quantifiable down to 0.0000015 mg/l, i.e. 1.5 ng/l. The permitted measurement uncertainty is 50 per cent.</div>
            </details>
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group">
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">Which standards describe PFAS analysis in water?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">In Germany the key standards are DIN 38407-42 for drinking, ground and surface water as well as treated wastewater, and DIN EN 17892 specifically for drinking water. Both use liquid chromatography with tandem mass spectrometry (LC-MS/MS).</div>
            </details>
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group">
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">Can a lab container house an accredited PFAS laboratory?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Yes, provided the room climate, power supply, gas supply, extract and choice of materials are designed for LC-MS/MS trace analysis. The container itself does not confer accreditation: the laboratory must agree a new measurement room or site with its accreditation body and reflect it in its quality management system.</div>
            </details>
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group">
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">Why are blank values so critical in PFAS analysis?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Measurements are made at a few nanograms per litre. Yet PFAS are also present in laboratory equipment and consumables, for example PTFE tubing or PTFE-lined closures. The achievable limit of quantification therefore depends directly on how low a laboratory can keep its blank values.</div>
            </details>
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group">
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">How quickly can additional PFAS laboratory space be created?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">A custom-designed Planexus lab container is typically manufactured in 8 to 14 weeks. Design, permits, installation, instrument set-up and coordination with the accreditation body come on top. Anyone who needs additional capacity by the PFAS-4 deadline on 12 January 2028 should start planning in 2026.</div>
            </details>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

